<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
        Downloading is not incidental to this app: the Android platform is a
        63 MB component fetched on first build, and M4 resolves dependencies
        straight from Google's and Maven Central's repositories. Without this
        the failure surfaces from inside a DNS lookup as a SecurityException,
        which reads as a bug in the resolver rather than a missing permission.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <!--
      The Logcat tab. An app sees only its own lines without this, so the tab
      could not show the log of the app the user just built, which runs under a
      different uid. With it declared, Android itself asks the user, one read
      at a time: "Allow AIDE-OS to access all device logs?" with "Allow
      one-time access" as the only yes. No root, no adb, no desktop.

      The cost is that every install carries an app that can ask, and the
      system dialog tells the user to allow it "only [for] apps you trust".
      That is why it sat out of the manifest for a milestone, with
      tools/logcat/FINDINGS.md as the deliverable instead. It is in now as a
      deliberate product decision, not because it became technically easier.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_LOGS" />

    <!--
      The last step of the loop this app exists for: build, install, run.

      ApkInstaller has always handled the refusal properly, checking
      canRequestPackageInstalls() and offering the user
      ACTION_MANAGE_UNKNOWN_APP_SOURCES. That offer was a dead end, because an
      app that has not declared this does not appear on that Settings page at
      all: the build produced a signed APK and then stopped, with a remedy that
      could not work.

      It went unnoticed because the install step has only ever been proven with
      privileges the app does not have. ApkInstallerTest flips appops to make
      canInstall() true, and the milestone's own acceptance test installs
      through shell `pm install` and says so in a comment: "what is being tested
      here is the APK, not the class."

      This is the permission R5 in docs/PLAN.md is about. Play scrutinises it,
      and the plan already treats F-Droid and a direct APK as the primary
      channels. The argument for declaring it is the one the project rests on:
      an IDE that cannot install what it compiles is not one.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />

    <!--
        D8 dexes a whole project's classes in this process, and the Kotlin front
        end will want considerably more when M4 wires it in. A default heap is
        sized for an app, not for a compiler holding a program's type graph.

        This is headroom, not isolation: an OOM here still takes the editor down
        with the build, and the unsaved buffers with it. The fix docs/PLAN.md
        asks for is a separate :build process behind a bound service, which this
        does not replace.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".AideApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:largeHeap="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.AIDEOS">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.AIDEOS"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <!--
                    Google's reverse-client-id scheme, not one we chose: an
                    Android OAuth client accepts only this form. Matched on
                    scheme alone because the redirect has no authority
                    (`scheme:/oauth2redirect`), and an intent filter cannot
                    match a path without a host.
                -->
                <data android:scheme="com.googleusercontent.apps.787312694223-4bhh6bk282u07553nc3iq1m7fuok6anh" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            Builds run here, not in the UI process. The Kotlin compiler's heap
            is the reason (risk R3): held in the app's process it competes with
            the editor and every open buffer, and an out-of-memory kill takes
            the user's unsaved work with a build they could have retried.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".build.BuildService"
            android:process=":build"
            android:exported="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>